From 59eca3ff97a7c538c20a4ae1d71183ad3c18b6ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tserdeiro <tserdeiro@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 07:56:08 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(git): gate optional commit hooks on their own auto_commit
 key

A fresh project registers all sixteen optional git.commit hooks
unconditionally in .specify/extensions.yml. auto_commit.default ships
false and every event's own auto_commit.<event>.enabled ships false
too (config-template.yml), so every one of those hooks was announced
by core commands as if it might do something -- agreeing to run the
announced /speckit.git.commit was a no-op, since the command itself
checks the same disabled config before deciding not to commit. Command
templates also skipped any hook carrying a non-empty condition
outright, so a condition alone could not have fixed this: nothing
evaluated it.

Give each git.commit hook the condition it needs to respect at run
time: condition: "config.auto_commit.<event>.enabled == 'true'", using
HookExecutor's existing config.<path> == 'value' grammar. Then make
templates honor it: every hook block in templates/commands/*.md
(before_ and after_, ten commands) now evaluates a hook's condition
the way HookExecutor._evaluate_condition does -- no condition means
executable; config.<path> is read from the hook's own
.specify/extensions/<extension>/<extension>-config.yml, env.<VAR> from
the process environment; anything else, or a config file that can't be
read, leaves the hook not executable -- instead of skipping any
conditioned hook outright. docs/reference/extensions.md documents the
same rule. The two mandatory hooks (git.initialize, git.feature) are
untouched; they carry no auto_commit key to gate on.

HookExecutor.should_execute_hook already evaluated this grammar
correctly against the extension's own git-config.yml, live, so turning
an event's auto-commit on later needs no reinstall. Two limits of the
expression remain: the grammar has no disjunction, so
auto_commit.default's "enable for all, then override" shorthand is not
honored (only the per-event key is), and a mistyped condition degrades
silently to never-executable.

Tests: the manifest declares the expected condition on every
git.commit entry and none on the two mandatory hooks; installing the
extension writes the condition into the registered before_plan entry,
and should_execute_hook is false under the shipped git-config.yml and
true once auto_commit.before_plan.enabled is set to true in it. A
parametrized test over templates/commands/*.md pins that the old
skip-unconditionally sentence is gone and the evaluation rule reads
identically across all twenty hook blocks.
